          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"A ship primarily symbolizes the Church as a vessel carrying believers through the dangers of the world toward salvation; secondary uses include individual life, commerce, wealth, pride, and worldly powers.\",\"book_summary\":\"A ship symbolizes any organized corporate polity—kingdom, city, or state—moving among the sea of peoples, with rulers as pilots and inhabitants as crew. The Church is treated as one possible Christ-headed instance rather than the exclusive referent.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Ship = the Church as a vessel of salvation.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Ship = an organized kingdom, city, or state.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_BROADENS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUPERSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The consensus centers the symbol on the Church, while the book extends it to secular cities, kingdoms, and states such as Tyre and Babylon. It consequently reclassifies Church-as-ship as a particular case—especially when Christ is its Head—within a broader political-corporate category.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no necessary contradiction because the Church can be understood as an organized corporate polity and therefore remain within the book's category. The disagreement is material at the boundary: the book denies the Church exclusive or primary control of the symbol and admits many non-ecclesial vessels.\",\"rationale\":\"After reduction, the readings share the idea of a ship as an organized human community under leadership, but differ in scope. The book preserves the ecclesial case while generalizing its referent from the Church to organized societies broadly. This is a substantive broadening rather than a replacement by an incompatible referent, so REFINED is more appropriate than DIVERGENT or NOVEL.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Ezekiel 27 strongly depicts the city-state Tyre through extended ship imagery and identifies inhabitants, pilots, military personnel, and merchants within that corporate image. Revelation 18 also connects maritime commerce with Babylon. However, several other references concern literal ships, and the printed citations alone do not establish the universal rule that a ship always signifies an organized polity or that its surrounding sea always signifies peoples.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
